I believe this is probably the key.  Nobody's going to solve very many
of Nethack's strategic decisions automatically, because they depend on
too many things.  Local tactics, however, are manageable.  And many
strategic decisions (which scrolls to carry? what ring to wear?) are
relatively unimportant to do *perfectly*, because simple rules do a
pretty good job already.

The basic strategy is to keep the code as simple as possible.  You
don't need to consider every option the game gives you.  (People
following conducts ignore a large chunk of them, for instance.)  It's
OK to ignore most possibilities and concentrate on making rules for
dealing with the few options that are most likely to succeed.

The simplest way I can think of to write a bot that might occasionally
do all right is as follows:

Play a wizard.  Restart until you have 18 int, a ring of slow
digestion, and a spellbook of identify.  Maybe a magic marker and
charging scroll too for good measure.  Reasons: slow digestion lets
you solve many problems with patience.  Dealing with unidentified
items is very complicated in general, so the spell saves a lot of
trouble.

Besides weapons and armor, save only the following items:
Wands of fire/lightning (for Elbereth), death, perhaps teleportation
and others
Scrolls of enchant armor, charging, genocide
Potions of [extra|full] healing
(also see invisible, gain ability until holy water is available)
Many rings (wear the best ones you have)
Amulet of reflection (don't lose that =oSD!)
bag, stethoscope, magic marker

Fight physically, don't cast spells.  It's too complicated to find
enough books and non-metal armor, especially since you have to be able
to fight physically anyhow when you run out of power.

Don't have a pet.  It's too hard to keep track of it, especially while
blind or hallucinating.

Some important tactical maneuvers that would need coding:

Movement.  Explore dark areas at full health.  Search before moving.
Search walls for secret doors if you can't find the downstairs.
Always keep a clear path to the upstairs.  Remember everything.

Eating.  Eat anything safe that you've just killed.  Carry some
rations.  Perhaps remove =oSD if satiated.  Eat poisonous things once
you have a unicorn horn (a challenge in itself).  Later, eat things
that might give you an intrinsic.  If you have enough food, consider
letting monsters spawn to get experience and items.

Fighting.  Stand toe to toe with few monsters, back into a corridor or
stairway to fight more.  The +2 Magicbane would be a sufficient weapon
for a patient bot.  Drink healing potions or return upstairs to heal
if you calculate that you might not win.  Consider special routines
for unusual monsters like unicorns and eels.

Sacrifice at altars.  You need to get Magicbane to fight, engrave, and
absorb curses (otherwise a dangerous and complicated part of the
endgame -- another good reason to play a wizard).  You also want
maximum luck after finding a luckstone.  This is possibly doable by
hanging around an altar waiting for monsters to spawn.

Shopping.  Identify everything in a shop, then buy what you want.
Don't bother trying to steal, because this involves either a pet or an
angry shopkeeper.

Identifying.  Try the spell whenever you get enough power and have an
unidentified item.  Once you get basic divination skill, don't leave a
level until you've identified every item in it except for inferior
weapons and armor.

Making holy water.  This is too important to skip, but unavoidably a
bit tricky.  The easiest way might go something like, wait for a wand
of digging; hit a fountain with it; drop vulnerable equipment; go
diving until your potions are all water; dip or pray them into holy
water.

Enchanting.  Be able to blank and write blessed scrolls of enchant
armor, and guess when it's useful to do so.

Wishing.  Gray or silver scale mail, boots of speed, gauntlets of
power, magic markers.

Special levels.  Sokoban, castle, endgame (ha!).

Plenty of things I'm surely forgetting.  The ones above are nontrivial
and could well turn out to be much harder than they look to me at the
moment.  And the wizard would definitely not win all the time, but
with some luck it might be possible to do well for a while.

Sometimes a bot will even have an advantage over a human player.  You
don't really need a cache when you can remember the location of
everything you've ever seen.  Mind flayers are not especially
dangerous.  You're never too impatient to go back up the stairs and
heal.

Now, this is not to say that *anyone* should actually do this, let
alone me.  Implementing I/O alone would be a major hassle.  And
without an implementation you can't prove that this or any other
approach will work.

But I do think there exist simple heuristics that have a good chance
of doing well in this limited case (W with +ID and =oSD).  And I'm
sure that the only way to solve the more general case would be to
gradually build more features onto a simpler program.  It's an
interesting thought experiment, anyway.

